BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//1AM Wisdom//Curated Year//EN
CALSCALE:GREGORIAN
METHOD:PUBLISH
X-WR-CALNAME:4AM Wisdom
X-WR-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260901@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260901T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260901T011200
SUMMARY:Silence is not empty
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\nIdea: Silence is not the absence of experience. Attention reveals events we habitually exclude as noise or nothing.\nContext: Cage's work on silence and listening; especially 4′33″ and his writings on sound.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260902@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260902T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260902T011200
SUMMARY:Can a game stay open?
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\nIdea: A finite game is played to conclude in victory. An infinite game is played so that play can continue. Ask what your present activity is trying to preserve: victory or possibility.\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260903@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260903T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260903T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness has degrees
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\nIdea: Places and things are not simply whole or fragmented. Their structure can intensify or diminish the life of the larger field around them.\nContext: Alexander's later work on centers and degrees of life; The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260904@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260904T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260904T011200
SUMMARY:Hope contradicts now
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\nIdea: Hope is not merely confidence that present trends will turn out well. It can contradict the apparent finality of the present by orienting life toward what is promised but not yet actual.\nContext: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260905@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260905T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260905T011200
SUMMARY:The margin may be center
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\nIdea: The grotesque can disclose realities that ordinary proportion conceals. Distortion is sometimes a way of making an invisible moral structure visible.\nContext: O'Connor's fiction and essays on the grotesque and the Christian novelist.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260906@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260906T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260906T011200
SUMMARY:What resists your map?
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\nIdea: We notice the content carried by a medium and overlook the environment the medium creates. Look for the part of experience your conceptual map makes difficult to perceive.\nContext: McLuhan's distinction between media content and the transformations produced by media themselves.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260907@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260907T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260907T011200
SUMMARY:Become singular
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\nIdea: A crowd can relieve the individual of responsibility. Becoming a self requires standing personally before the demand one recognizes as true rather than borrowing conviction from numbers.\nContext: Kierkegaard's writings on 'the single individual' and the crowd.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260908@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T011200
SUMMARY:The wound can be a door
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\nIdea: What the conscious personality rejects may return as symptom, projection, or disturbance. The troublesome material can become an entrance into a larger self if it is encountered rather than merely expelled.\nContext: Jung's work on shadow and individuation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260909@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260909T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260909T011200
SUMMARY:Usefulness has a shadow
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\nIdea: A thing need not justify itself by immediate utility. Imagination, story, play, and beauty may enlarge the person who later decides what counts as useful.\nContext: MacDonald's essays on imagination and fairy tales.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260910@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260910T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260910T011200
SUMMARY:Less can make more possible
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\nIdea: Good design does not merely add resources. It can reorganize relationships so that less matter and energy support greater capability.\nContext: Fuller's idea of doing more with less and ephemeralization.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260911@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260911T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260911T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can unmake wonder
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\nIdea: In the divine life, genuine knowledge does not terminate desire. Encounter enlarges the capacity for further encounter; fulfillment can deepen rather than extinguish longing.\nContext: Gregory's account of endless progress, often called epektasis, especially in The Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260912@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260912T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260912T011200
SUMMARY:Remove one certainty
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite\nIdea: Negation can be intellectually productive. To say what ultimate reality is not can loosen concepts that have quietly become substitutes for what they attempt to describe.\nContext: The Mystical Theology and the apophatic tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260913@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260913T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260913T011200
SUMMARY:What owns your attention?
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\nIdea: A recurring thought is not merely something you think. Repetition can give it a kind of agency over attention and action. Observe the thought's pattern before arguing with its content.\nContext: Evagrius's analysis of logismoi, recurring tempting or obsessive thought-patterns.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260914@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260914T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260914T011200
SUMMARY:A cell can teach
DESCRIPTION:Desert Fathers, especially Abba Moses and the sayings tradition\nIdea: Remaining in one place can expose evasions that movement disguises. Constraint sometimes becomes an instrument of perception.\nContext: The Sayings of the Desert Fathers repeatedly associate staying in one's cell with learning and transformation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260915@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260915T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260915T011200
SUMMARY:Love alters perception
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\nIdea: Attention is not neutral inspection. The quality of attention can make another person's reality perceptible without immediately converting that person into a project, category, or instrument.\nContext: Weil's writings on attention, affliction, and love of neighbor.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260916@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260916T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260916T011200
SUMMARY:The ordinary has depth
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\nIdea: A small, vulnerable thing can disclose an unexpectedly large metaphysical horizon. Scale and significance need not correspond.\nContext: Julian's vision of a small object 'the quantity of a hazelnut' held in her hand, used to contemplate creation and divine love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260917@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260917T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260917T011200
SUMMARY:Unknow what you know
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\nIdea: Spiritual and intellectual possession can become an obstacle. Detachment includes relinquishing cherished images of truth when those images prevent encounter with what exceeds them.\nContext: Eckhart's sermons on detachment, poverty of spirit, and knowing God beyond images.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260918@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260918T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260918T011200
SUMMARY:Opposites can coincide
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\nIdea: At the limits of ordinary comparison, oppositions generated by finite concepts may cease to behave as oppositions. Contradiction can sometimes mark the boundary of a conceptual instrument rather than the boundary of reality.\nContext: Cusa's 'coincidence of opposites' and learned ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260919@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260919T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260919T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can demand labor
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\nIdea: Beauty need not be an escape from difficult making. The finished image can conceal revision, discipline, failure, and return; enchantment and workmanship are not opposites.\nContext: Rossetti's intertwined practice of poetry, painting, revision, and reworking.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260920@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260920T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260920T011200
SUMMARY:Renunciation makes room
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\nIdea: Refusal is not always mere deprivation. Giving something up can create a form of interior space in which another allegiance, perception, or desire becomes possible.\nContext: A recurring structure in Rossetti's devotional poetry, where desire, restraint, loss, and transformed desire interact.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260921@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T011200
SUMMARY:Is the joke still a joke?
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\nIdea: A performance can destabilize the boundary between event and representation. When the audience cannot locate the frame, its own expectations become part of the material.\nContext: Kaufman's deliberate blurring of character, prank, performance, sincerity, and audience complicity.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260923@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260923T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260923T011200
SUMMARY:Do not canonize reality
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nWhat exists deserves attention\, not worship. Hope keeps actuality from becoming the measure of possibility.\n\nContext: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260924@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260924T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260924T011200
SUMMARY:Silence can be social
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nSilence can become a form of solidarity when it clears space to perceive suffering without immediately turning it into self-display.\n\nContext: later writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260925@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T011200
SUMMARY:The maker serves the work
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nArt has its own internal demands. The maker's sincerity does not excuse a work that has not found its proper form.\n\nContext: Art and Scholasticism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260926@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260926T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260926T011200
SUMMARY:A system can enslave
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nBlake distrusts systems that become substitutes for perception\, especially when inherited categories begin dictating what can be seen.\n\nContext: prophetic writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260927@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260927T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260927T011200
SUMMARY:Let sounds be themselves
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nListening can loosen the impulse to make every sound serve intention\, expression\, or hierarchy.\n\nContext: Silence.\n\nConnection: Merton and Cage both found that silence is not blank. For Merton it can strip away performed identity\; for Cage it reveals the sound-world already happening.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260928@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260928T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260928T011200
SUMMARY:Anger clouds contemplation
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nAnger is not merely a moral fault\; it can alter the field of perception so that certain realities become difficult to see.\n\nContext: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260929@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260929T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260929T011200
SUMMARY:A story need not explain
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nA story can work on perception before the reader can translate its effect into a proposition.\n\nContext: The Fantastic Imagination.\n\nConnection: MacDonald and Blake both refuse to treat imagination as decorative unreality. For both\, imaginative vision can expose possibilities the ordinary habits of perception conceal.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260930@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260930T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260930T011200
SUMMARY:Schooling can hide learning
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nInstitutional certification can become confused with learning itself\, making informal competence socially invisible.\n\nContext: Deschooling Society.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261001@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261001T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261001T011200
SUMMARY:The joke may be the doubt
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nSometimes the comic event is not a punchline but the audience's inability to decide whether a joke is occurring.\n\nContext: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261002@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261002T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261002T011200
SUMMARY:Carry your leaking bag
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nIn a famous saying\, Moses carries a leaking container to dramatize how one's own faults can trail behind unnoticed while judging another.\n\nContext: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261003@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261003T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261003T011200
SUMMARY:Sincerity can be staged
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performance can make sincerity undecidable without making it meaningless.\n\nContext: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261004@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261004T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261004T011200
SUMMARY:Design can outrun politics
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nSome problems framed as conflicts over scarcity may change when design alters the resource conditions underneath them.\n\nContext: World Game and design science.\n\nConnection: Fuller trusts redesign to unlock abundance\; Illich worries that systems can become counterproductive even when technically successful. Their disagreement is a useful brake on either thinker alone.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261005@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261005T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261005T011200
SUMMARY:Attention waits empty
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nReal attention does not rush to fill a question with the first available answer.\n\nContext: Reflections on the Right Use of School Studies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261006@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261006T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261006T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can demand labor
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nEnchantment and painstaking revision are not opposites\; an apparently immediate image may be the product of repeated return.\n\nContext: poetry and painting practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261007@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261007T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261007T011200
SUMMARY:The many can cohere
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nMultiplicity can belong to order without requiring sameness.\n\nContext: cosmic theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261008@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T011200
SUMMARY:The grotesque can see
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nDistortion can reveal moral structures that polite realism leaves comfortably invisible.\n\nContext: Mystery and Manners.\n\nConnection: O'Connor and Abba Moses both distrust moral superiority. One uses grotesque fiction\, the other comic desert gestures\, to expose the person who can see everyone else's fault.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261009@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T011200
SUMMARY:Darkness can be passage
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nNot every loss of orientation is mere failure\; some transitions cannot preserve the old map.\n\nContext: Dark Night.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261010@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261010T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261010T011200
SUMMARY:God has a future
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nMoltmann's theology treats God not merely as the eternal ground behind events but as the one toward whose promised future history moves.\n\nContext: The Coming of God.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261011@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261011T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261011T011200
SUMMARY:Centers make centers
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA strong center is supported by other centers around it\; coherence is relational rather than isolated.\n\nContext: The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261012@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261012T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261012T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can contract
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nDesire can create a center by contraction\, producing both form and the risk of enclosure.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261013@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261013T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261013T011200
SUMMARY:Knowledge has plural modes
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nScientific\, philosophical\, artistic\, and contemplative knowing need not compete as if every truth must use the same instrument.\n\nContext: Degrees of Knowledge.\n\nConnection: Maritain's sense that form can be intrinsically intelligible meets Alexander's insistence that good form is relational and alive\, not merely imposed from outside.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261014@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T011200
SUMMARY:Memory is a vast room
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nThe self encounters itself through a memory it uses constantly but cannot fully survey.\n\nContext: Confessions\, Book X.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261015@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261015T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261015T011200
SUMMARY:Love outlasts the wound
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nA wound can remain real without being granted the final word about the whole.\n\nContext: Revelations.\n\nConnection: Moltmann and Julian both let hope resist the apparent finality of suffering\, but neither needs to pretend the wound is unreal.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261016@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261016T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261016T011200
SUMMARY:Attention alters noise
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nThe difference between signal and noise is partly a decision about what deserves attention.\n\nContext: writings and compositional practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261017@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261017T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261017T011200
SUMMARY:Distraction is still prayer
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nA wandering mind does not automatically invalidate prayer\; the struggle with distraction can itself reveal how attention works.\n\nContext: Way of Perfection.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261018@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261018T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261018T011200
SUMMARY:Speed can consume distance
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nFaster transport can reorganize settlement and expectation so radically that gains in speed create new forms of compulsory travel.\n\nContext: Energy and Equity.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261019@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261019T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261019T011200
SUMMARY:Can certainty choose?
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nExistential commitment begins where detached calculation can no longer make the decision for you.\n\nContext: Concluding Unscientific Postscript.\n\nConnection: Augustine's divided will and Kierkegaard's analysis of despair both make the self a task rather than a transparent possession.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261020@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261020T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261020T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness has degrees
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA place can become more or less alive as relationships among its centers strengthen or weaken.\n\nContext: The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Maritain's sense that form can be intrinsically intelligible meets Alexander's insistence that good form is relational and alive\, not merely imposed from outside.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261021@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261021T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261021T011200
SUMMARY:Consent is not passivity
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nConsent can name a disciplined receptivity that differs from both domination and mere resignation.\n\nContext: Gravity and Grace.\n\nConnection: Weil's attention and Cage's listening meet in a disciplined refusal to decide too quickly what matters. Weil makes it ethical\; Cage makes it acoustic.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261022@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261022T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261022T011200
SUMMARY:A freak can clarify norms
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nThe socially anomalous figure can reveal how strange the supposedly normal order already is.\n\nContext: Mystery and Manners.\n\nConnection: Weil's attention and O'Connor's violence look opposed\, yet both are suspicious of the self-protective stories by which a person avoids reality.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261023@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T011200
SUMMARY:Humility needs discernment
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nSelf-abasement and humility are not identical\; humility requires truth rather than theatrical self-contempt.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261024@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261024T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261024T011200
SUMMARY:Roots are relational
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nHuman beings need meaningful participation in inherited and local worlds\, not merely abstract rights or material provision.\n\nContext: The Need for Roots.\n\nConnection: Weil's attention and O'Connor's violence look opposed\, yet both are suspicious of the self-protective stories by which a person avoids reality.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261025@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261025T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261025T011200
SUMMARY:Let the image go
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nDetachment can include relinquishing even spiritually valuable images when they become objects of possession.\n\nContext: sermons on detachment.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261026@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261026T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261026T011200
SUMMARY:The dream compensates
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nA dream can counterbalance the waking attitude by presenting material consciousness has neglected or exaggerated.\n\nContext: dream psychology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261027@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261027T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261027T011200
SUMMARY:Speed changes institutions
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nWhen communication accelerates enough\, institutions built around delay and distance can change category rather than merely operate faster.\n\nContext: Understanding Media.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261028@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261028T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261028T011200
SUMMARY:Less can enable more
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nDesign can reorganize relationships so that less material and energy support greater capability.\n\nContext: ephemeralization.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261029@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261029T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261029T011200
SUMMARY:Patterns are relations
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA useful pattern is not a decorative motif but a recurring relation among forces in a situation.\n\nContext: A Pattern Language.\n\nConnection: Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261030@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261030T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261030T011200
SUMMARY:Solitude has company
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nWithdrawal from social noise does not remove conflict\; it can make internal voices newly audible.\n\nContext: Life and sayings tradition.\n\nConnection: Anthony and Syncletica both distrust geographical solutions to interior problems. The desert does not magically remove what the traveler brings with them.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261031@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261031T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261031T011200
SUMMARY:Projection hides ownership
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nWhat appears to belong entirely to another person may partly reveal material the observer has not recognized as their own.\n\nContext: projection.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261101@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261101T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261101T011200
SUMMARY:Paradox tests thought
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nA paradox can expose an assumption so familiar that ordinary formulation leaves it invisible.\n\nContext: essays and apologetics.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261102@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T011200
SUMMARY:Silence is not empty
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nSilence becomes crowded when attention stops deciding in advance which sounds count.\n\nContext: 4′33″ and writings on silence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261103@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T011200
SUMMARY:The obvious can be strange
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nThe most familiar fact may be hardest to see precisely because it has ceased to surprise us.\n\nContext: essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261104@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T011200
SUMMARY:A monastery can be worldly
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nWithdrawal from ordinary society does not guarantee freedom from ambition\, status\, or self-deception.\n\nContext: journals and monastic writings.\n\nConnection: Merton and Cage both found that silence is not blank. For Merton it can strip away performed identity\; for Cage it reveals the sound-world already happening.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261105@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261105T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261105T011200
SUMMARY:Individuation is relation
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nBecoming oneself is not withdrawal into private uniqueness\; it requires a changed relation to both unconscious material and other people.\n\nContext: individuation writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261106@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261106T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261106T011200
SUMMARY:Art knows by making
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nArtistic intelligence can know its object through disciplined making rather than detached inspection alone.\n\nContext: Creative Intuition in Art and Poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261107@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261107T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261107T011200
SUMMARY:Anxiety reveals freedom
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nAnxiety can disclose possibility itself: the unsettling fact that one can become otherwise.\n\nContext: The Concept of Anxiety.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261108@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261108T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261108T011200
SUMMARY:Professional need is sticky
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nOnce a need is professionally defined\, institutions gain an incentive to reproduce the category of deficiency they exist to serve.\n\nContext: social criticism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261109@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261109T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261109T011200
SUMMARY:What owns attention?
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nA recurring thought can organize perception and action before argument ever begins.\n\nContext: Praktikos.\n\nConnection: Jung's complexes and Evagrius's recurring thoughts belong to different psychologies\, yet both treat mental events as patterns with a degree of autonomy rather than transparent expressions of the conscious self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261110@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261110T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261110T011200
SUMMARY:Restoration widens hope
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nJudgment and transformation can be imagined within a cosmic horizon larger than immediate retribution.\n\nContext: speculative theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261111@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261111T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261111T011200
SUMMARY:The knower has limits
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nLearned ignorance is not anti-intellectual. It is intelligence becoming exact about the limits of its own instrument.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261112@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T011200
SUMMARY:A character can escape
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performed persona can acquire enough continuity that the distinction between actor\, character\, and public event becomes unstable.\n\nContext: Tony Clifton performances.\n\nConnection: Kaufman destabilizes the audience's certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard destabilizes the individual's certainty about borrowed conviction. Both make the observer responsible for deciding where to stand.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261113@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261113T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261113T011200
SUMMARY:Perfection can move
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nPerfection need not mean static completion\; it can be endless growth into an inexhaustible good.\n\nContext: epektasis.\n\nConnection: Gregory's endless ascent and Cusa's learned ignorance both make intellectual progress compatible with increasing awareness of inexhaustibility.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261114@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261114T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261114T011200
SUMMARY:Reputation is poor armor
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nA spiritual reputation can become another identity to defend\, making criticism more dangerous to the ego than failure itself.\n\nContext: sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261115@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261115T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261115T011200
SUMMARY:The future can interrupt
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nThe future is not only what arrives later. Anticipated possibility can break the spell of what currently looks necessary.\n\nContext: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261116@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261116T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261116T011200
SUMMARY:Loneliness is not solitude
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nPhysical aloneness can either intensify fragmentation or become a disciplined form of presence\; the difference is not automatic.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261117@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can be unsettling
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nSensuous beauty can carry melancholy\, fixation\, and danger rather than functioning as uncomplicated consolation.\n\nContext: poetry and painting.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261118@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261118T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261118T011200
SUMMARY:Learn your ignorance
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nRecognizing the structure of one's ignorance can be a positive form of knowledge rather than mere deficiency.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.\n\nConnection: Gregory's endless ascent and Cusa's learned ignorance both make intellectual progress compatible with increasing awareness of inexhaustibility.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261119@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261119T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261119T011200
SUMMARY:Will creates a center
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nWill gathers diffuse possibility into a center of action\, creating both agency and the possibility of self-enclosure.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261120@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261120T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261120T011200
SUMMARY:Humility is accurate sight
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nHumility is not self-hatred but increasingly accurate perception of oneself in relation to reality.\n\nContext: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261121@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261121T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261121T011200
SUMMARY:Resurrection is protest
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nResurrection means that death is real without being granted the authority to define the whole.\n\nContext: Theology of Hope\; The Crucified God.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261122@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261122T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261122T011200
SUMMARY:Scarcity can be produced
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nInstitutions can manufacture dependence by redefining ordinary capacities as deficiencies requiring professional supply.\n\nContext: social criticism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261123@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261123T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261123T011200
SUMMARY:A poem can enact
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nA poem can perform the movement it describes\, making form part of meaning.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261124@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261124T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261124T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can keep growing
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nFulfillment need not extinguish desire. Encounter can enlarge the capacity for further encounter.\n\nContext: Life of Moses.\n\nConnection: Gregory's endless ascent and Cusa's learned ignorance both make intellectual progress compatible with increasing awareness of inexhaustibility.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261125@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261125T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261125T011200
SUMMARY:Desire needs empty space
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nAn overfilled interior life leaves little room for anything not already anticipated by existing preference.\n\nContext: Ascent of Mount Carmel.\n\nConnection: Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity with absence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261126@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261126T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261126T011200
SUMMARY:Desire colors the scene
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nAn image can reveal as much about the structure of longing as about the object apparently represented.\n\nContext: poetry and painting.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261127@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261127T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261127T011200
SUMMARY:Love reaches farther
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nDesire and love may reach toward what analytic comprehension cannot contain.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261128@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261128T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261128T011200
SUMMARY:Imagination is a faculty
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nImagination is not merely fantasy opposed to reality\; it is a mode through which reality becomes humanly apprehensible.\n\nContext: prophetic writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261129@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261129T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261129T011200
SUMMARY:Shape changes reading
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nVisual arrangement can alter tempo\, emphasis\, and conceptual relation before the words are paraphrased.\n\nContext: pattern poems.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261130@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261130T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261130T011200
SUMMARY:Tools reshape users
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nA tool does not merely extend an ability. Repeated use can reorganize the sensorium and the user who uses it.\n\nContext: Understanding Media.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261201@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261201T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261201T011200
SUMMARY:Prudence is practical truth
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nPractical wisdom concerns truth in action: not merely knowing a rule\, but judging what this situation asks.\n\nContext: moral philosophy.\n\nConnection: Maritain's claim that art knows through making becomes concrete in Herbert\, where poetic form does intellectual work that paraphrase cannot replace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261202@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261202T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261202T011200
SUMMARY:A symbol exceeds a sign
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nA living symbol does not merely encode a known meaning. It gathers meanings that cannot yet be stated exhaustively.\n\nContext: symbols and transformation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261203@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261203T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261203T011200
SUMMARY:Repair the larger whole
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA good local change improves not only its target but the larger field of relationships around it.\n\nContext: The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Maritain's sense that form can be intrinsically intelligible meets Alexander's insistence that good form is relational and alive\, not merely imposed from outside.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261204@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261204T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261204T011200
SUMMARY:Spaceship Earth has no edge
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nThinking of Earth as a shared vehicle changes the intuition that waste can simply be thrown 'away.'\n\nContext: Operating Manual for Spaceship Earth.\n\nConnection: Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261205@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T011200
SUMMARY:Help can disable
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nProfessionalized help can sometimes erode the capacities and relationships it intends to support.\n\nContext: Deschooling Society\; Medical Nemesis.\n\nConnection: McLuhan asks how a medium reshapes its user\; Illich asks when a tool or institution crosses the threshold from serving human capacity to disabling it. Together they make 'what does this tool do?' a much larger question.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261206@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261206T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261206T011200
SUMMARY:Poverty can be inward
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nInner poverty can mean freedom even from the demand to possess one's own spiritual state.\n\nContext: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261207@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261207T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261207T011200
SUMMARY:Content is the decoy
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nThe obvious content of a medium can distract from the structural changes the medium itself produces.\n\nContext: Understanding Media.\n\nConnection: McLuhan asks how a medium reshapes its user\; Illich asks when a tool or institution crosses the threshold from serving human capacity to disabling it. Together they make 'what does this tool do?' a much larger question.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261208@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261208T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261208T011200
SUMMARY:Nothingness can free
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nBecoming 'nothing' names a release from self-importance and possession\, not the destruction of personhood.\n\nContext: sermons on poverty.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261209@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261209T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261209T011200
SUMMARY:Silence can be mercy
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nSilence can sometimes protect another person from the violence of one's need to correct\, classify\, or prevail.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261210@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261210T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261210T011200
SUMMARY:Freedom survives providence
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nDivine providence need not erase creaturely freedom\; moral development requires real participation.\n\nContext: On First Principles.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261211@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261211T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261211T011200
SUMMARY:The medium acts first
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nA medium changes habits\, scale\, pace\, and relation before its explicit content is considered.\n\nContext: Understanding Media.\n\nConnection: McLuhan asks how a medium reshapes its user\; Illich asks when a tool or institution crosses the threshold from serving human capacity to disabling it. Together they make 'what does this tool do?' a much larger question.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261212@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261212T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261212T011200
SUMMARY:Courage belongs inward
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nInterior work can require practical courage because it unsettles identities and habits that once organized the self.\n\nContext: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261213@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261213T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261213T011200
SUMMARY:Irony creates distance
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nIrony can loosen the grip of an identity or social script by creating distance from what previously seemed self-evident.\n\nContext: The Concept of Irony.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261214@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261214T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261214T011200
SUMMARY:Fairy tales teach limits
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nFairy tales make arbitrary limits vivid\, and thereby dramatize the fact that freedom always takes shape within conditions.\n\nContext: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261215@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261215T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261215T011200
SUMMARY:Contraries make progress
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nCreative movement may require contraries rather than the premature elimination of tension.\n\nContext: The Marriage of Heaven and Hell.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261216@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261216T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261216T011200
SUMMARY:Every name is provisional
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nEven the best theological name must finally be denied if it begins to function as an adequate definition.\n\nContext: Divine Names\; Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261217@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261217T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261217T011200
SUMMARY:Enter the unknowing
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nDiscursive thought can reach a point where further conceptual accumulation obstructs contemplation.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261218@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261218T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261218T011200
SUMMARY:Remove one certainty
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nNegation can free thought from mistaking its concepts for the reality those concepts attempt to name.\n\nContext: Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261219@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261219T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261219T011200
SUMMARY:Play changes the player
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nThe deepest games are not simply activities one performs\; participation alters the identity from which one plays.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261220@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261220T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261220T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can be redirected
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nPassion need not simply be annihilated\; desire can be transformed by changing what and how it loves.\n\nContext: ascetical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261221@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261221T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261221T011200
SUMMARY:Appearance is an event
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nAppearance need not be a veil hiding reality. Appearing is itself one of the ways reality happens.\n\nContext: phenomenological writings.\n\nConnection: Carse and Fink both take play seriously enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes continuation\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261222@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261222T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261222T011200
SUMMARY:Friendship can be method
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nPrayer can be understood less as technique than as sustained friendship\, changing its logic from performance to relation.\n\nContext: Life.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261223@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261223T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261223T011200
SUMMARY:The future needs players
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nAn infinite game has no point without future participants. Its ethics therefore include preserving conditions for people not yet present.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.\n\nConnection: Carse and Fink both take play seriously enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes continuation\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261224@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261224T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261224T011200
SUMMARY:Boredom can be active
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nWithholding conventional payoff can make an audience newly aware of its own impatience\, entitlement\, and interpretive labor.\n\nContext: performance practice.\n\nConnection: Kaufman's refusal to clarify whether the performance is 'really' a performance has an odd kinship with apophatic refusal: certainty about the frame is withheld\, though for radically different ends.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261225@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261225T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261225T011200
SUMMARY:Innocence is not ignorance
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nInnocence and experience are states of perception\, not simply chronological stages one leaves behind forever.\n\nContext: Songs of Innocence and of Experience.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261226@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261226T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261226T011200
SUMMARY:Illness changes practice
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nAscetic practice must respond to actual bodies rather than to abstract ideals of toughness.\n\nContext: sayings attributed to Syncletica.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261227@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261227T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261227T011200
SUMMARY:Energy resists cages
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nVitality can be distorted when moral systems treat energy itself as the enemy rather than asking what form it takes.\n\nContext: The Marriage of Heaven and Hell.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261228@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261228T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261228T011200
SUMMARY:Refusal can be creative
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nSaying no can be a positive act of form-making when refusal protects another commitment.\n\nContext: devotional poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261229@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261229T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261229T011200
SUMMARY:Goodness may look strange
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nMoral transformation can initially feel alien because it conflicts with the self's established picture of what fulfillment should be.\n\nContext: novels and fairy tales.\n\nConnection: MacDonald and Blake both refuse to treat imagination as decorative unreality. For both\, imaginative vision can expose possibilities the ordinary habits of perception conceal.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261230@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261230T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261230T011200
SUMMARY:Scripture has depths
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA text may yield different levels of meaning as the reader and the question change.\n\nContext: On First Principles and homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261231@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261231T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261231T011200
SUMMARY:Maps can change decisions
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nA good map can make planetary interdependence perceptible enough to alter practical judgment.\n\nContext: Dymaxion map.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270101@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270101T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270101T011200
SUMMARY:Stay with the struggle
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nLeaving a difficult situation can sometimes preserve the very pattern one hoped to escape.\n\nContext: desert tradition.\n\nConnection: Anthony and Syncletica both distrust geographical solutions to interior problems. The desert does not magically remove what the traveler brings with them.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270102@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270102T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270102T011200
SUMMARY:Limits can free action
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nChosen limits can protect competence\, conviviality\, and local agency that unlimited systems tend to absorb.\n\nContext: Tools for Conviviality.\n\nConnection: Fuller trusts redesign to unlock abundance\; Illich worries that systems can become counterproductive even when technically successful. Their disagreement is a useful brake on either thinker alone.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270103@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270103T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270103T011200
SUMMARY:Grace disrupts
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nTransformation may first appear as interruption\, humiliation\, violence to self-image\, or loss of control.\n\nContext: fiction and essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270104@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270104T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270104T011200
SUMMARY:Mystery resists slogans
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nA story can embody mystery without reducing it to a detachable moral proposition.\n\nContext: Mystery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270105@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270105T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270105T011200
SUMMARY:The world is no object
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nThe world is not one more thing among things. It is the horizon within which things can appear at all.\n\nContext: phenomenological writings on world.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270106@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270106T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270106T011200
SUMMARY:Humility can be concrete
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nHumility becomes credible when enacted in situations where status\, injury\, and recognition are actually at stake.\n\nContext: sayings tradition.\n\nConnection: O'Connor and Abba Moses both distrust moral superiority. One uses grotesque fiction\, the other comic desert gestures\, to expose the person who can see everyone else's fault.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270107@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270107T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270107T011200
SUMMARY:The infinite breaks scale
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nFinite measures cannot simply be extrapolated until they become adequate to the infinite.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.\n\nConnection: Cusa's geometrical metaphors and Fuller's structural thinking share a fascination with how abstract form can reorganize intuition\, though one aims at learned ignorance and the other at design.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270108@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270108T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270108T011200
SUMMARY:Do not flee too quickly
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nChanging places does not necessarily change the passions carried from place to place.\n\nContext: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.\n\nConnection: Anthony and Syncletica both distrust geographical solutions to interior problems. The desert does not magically remove what the traveler brings with them.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270109@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270109T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270109T011200
SUMMARY:The ground is deeper
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nThe deepest ground of the self may not coincide with the familiar ego that narrates itself.\n\nContext: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270110@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270110T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270110T011200
SUMMARY:Unknow what you know
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nPossession of an idea can obstruct encounter when the idea becomes a substitute for what it names.\n\nContext: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270111@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270111T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270111T011200
SUMMARY:Love can contain paradox
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nContradictions that seem irresolvable at one level can be held within a larger claim about love without being cheaply explained away.\n\nContext: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270112@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270112T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270112T011200
SUMMARY:Gratitude begins in excess
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nGratitude can arise from perceiving existence as gift before calculating whether circumstances meet expectations.\n\nContext: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270113@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270113T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270113T011200
SUMMARY:Love outruns calculation
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nLove becomes recognizably merciful when it gives up the fantasy that every good must be proportioned to deserving.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.\n\nConnection: Julian and Isaac share a startling confidence in mercy without making suffering imaginary. Both press compassion beyond the punitive instincts of ordinary moral accounting.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270114@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270114T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270114T011200
SUMMARY:Medicine can create illness
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nA system devoted to health can create new forms of harm when intervention\, dependency\, and medicalization exceed useful thresholds.\n\nContext: Medical Nemesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270115@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270115T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270115T011200
SUMMARY:Literal sense is a door
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA literal reading can be genuine without exhausting the possible meanings of a text.\n\nContext: biblical exegesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270116@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270116T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270116T011200
SUMMARY:Early fervor can mislead
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nEnthusiasm at the beginning of a practice can conceal whether it has become durable enough to survive boredom and difficulty.\n\nContext: sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270117@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270117T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270117T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty is not prettiness
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nBeauty concerns a radiance of form apprehended in things\; it is not reducible to charm\, polish\, or agreeable subject matter.\n\nContext: Art and Scholasticism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270118@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270118T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270118T011200
SUMMARY:A difficulty may be placed
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA textual difficulty can provoke deeper reading rather than simply signal failure.\n\nContext: On First Principles.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270119@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270119T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270119T011200
SUMMARY:A part can mediate
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nA local relation can participate in reconciling larger divisions rather than remaining merely local.\n\nContext: Ambigua.\n\nConnection: Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion meet in a refusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270120@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270120T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270120T011200
SUMMARY:Old media become art
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nWhen a medium is displaced\, features once taken for granted can become newly visible and aestheticized.\n\nContext: Laws of Media.\n\nConnection: McLuhan asks how a medium reshapes its user\; Illich asks when a tool or institution crosses the threshold from serving human capacity to disabling it. Together they make 'what does this tool do?' a much larger question.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270121@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270121T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270121T011200
SUMMARY:Force turns persons to things
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nViolence can reduce a person\, socially and imaginatively\, toward the condition of an object.\n\nContext: The Iliad\, or the Poem of Force.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270122@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270122T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270122T011200
SUMMARY:Meaning can arrive late
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nAn experience may remain partially unintelligible for years before a later insight reorganizes it.\n\nContext: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270123@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270123T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270123T011200
SUMMARY:Faith risks the absurd
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nFaith is not the elimination of uncertainty but a mode of commitment that cannot be secured by objective guarantees.\n\nContext: Fear and Trembling.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270124@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270124T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270124T011200
SUMMARY:Sanity can hold extremes
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nA sane synthesis may preserve two strong truths in tension rather than weakening each until conflict disappears.\n\nContext: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270125@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270125T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270125T011200
SUMMARY:Letting go is active
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nDetachment is not indifference but a disciplined openness that refuses to clutch at outcomes\, identities\, and consolations.\n\nContext: sermons and treatises.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270126@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270126T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270126T011200
SUMMARY:Tenderness can be strong
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nMercy need not be sentimentality\; it can demand a more difficult refusal to reduce a person to an offense.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.\n\nConnection: Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion meet in a refusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270127@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270127T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270127T011200
SUMMARY:Precision always escapes
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nFinite measurement can improve indefinitely without becoming identical to perfect precision.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270128@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270128T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270128T011200
SUMMARY:Rules can serve change
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nRules need not merely constrain play. In an open game they can be altered to preserve the possibility of continuing.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270129@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270129T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270129T011200
SUMMARY:Concepts can become clouds
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nThought can obscure as well as illuminate when it multiplies representations faster than attention deepens.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270130@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270130T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270130T011200
SUMMARY:Where is the frame?
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nWhen an audience cannot locate the boundary between performance and event\, its expectations become part of the work.\n\nContext: performance practice.\n\nConnection: Kaufman destabilizes the audience's certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard destabilizes the individual's certainty about borrowed conviction. Both make the observer responsible for deciding where to stand.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270131@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270131T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270131T011200
SUMMARY:Imagination is serious
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nImagination can enlarge the field in which usefulness itself is later judged.\n\nContext: The Fantastic Imagination.\n\nConnection: Chesterton inherits much from MacDonald's imaginative Christianity: wonder is not childish simplification but a discipline for seeing the ordinary as contingent and strange.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270201@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270201T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270201T011200
SUMMARY:We inhabit more than tasks
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nHuman existence cannot be exhausted by projects and functions\; world-experience exceeds the inventory of what we are doing.\n\nContext: existential phenomenology.\n\nConnection: Carse and Fink both take play seriously enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes continuation\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270202@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270202T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270202T011200
SUMMARY:Distinguish to unite
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nA good distinction need not fragment reality. It can make deeper forms of unity intelligible.\n\nContext: Degrees of Knowledge.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270203@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270203T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270203T011200
SUMMARY:Virtue needs friction
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nResistance can expose and strengthen capacities that ease leaves dormant.\n\nContext: Sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270204@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270204T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270204T011200
SUMMARY:A tool should leave room
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nA convivial tool expands the user's capacity to act without requiring expert control over the whole field.\n\nContext: Tools for Conviviality.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270205@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270205T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270205T011200
SUMMARY:Duration can be material
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nTime is not merely a container for sounds\; duration itself can become one of composition's primary materials.\n\nContext: compositional practice.\n\nConnection: Cage's silence and the Cloud author's unknowing both change attention by removing expected content. One opens the ear\; the other interrupts conceptual possession.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270206@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270206T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270206T011200
SUMMARY:Devotion can be witty
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nIntellectual play and devotional seriousness need not be opposites\; wit can become a mode of exact attention.\n\nContext: The Temple.\n\nConnection: Maritain's claim that art knows through making becomes concrete in Herbert\, where poetic form does intellectual work that paraphrase cannot replace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270207@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270207T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270207T011200
SUMMARY:Opposites can coincide
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nAt the limits of finite comparison\, an opposition may expose the limits of the measuring concept itself.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270208@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270208T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270208T011200
SUMMARY:The object can instruct
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nAn altar\, pulley\, collar\, flower\, or window can become a conceptual instrument rather than mere ornament.\n\nContext: The Temple.\n\nConnection: Maritain's claim that art knows through making becomes concrete in Herbert\, where poetic form does intellectual work that paraphrase cannot replace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270209@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270209T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270209T011200
SUMMARY:Practice reveals doctrine
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nWhat a person actually practices can disclose their operative theology more sharply than their explicit claims.\n\nContext: Macarian tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270210@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270210T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270210T011200
SUMMARY:Meaning can constellate
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nEvents can gather subjective meaning around an activated psychic pattern even when no simple causal story explains their resonance.\n\nContext: synchronicity and complex theory.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270211@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270211T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270211T011200
SUMMARY:Decreate the center
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nThe self's demand to occupy the center of reality can be loosened so that what is not-self becomes more perceptible.\n\nContext: Gravity and Grace.\n\nConnection: Weil's attention and Cage's listening meet in a disciplined refusal to decide too quickly what matters. Weil makes it ethical\; Cage makes it acoustic.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270212@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270212T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270212T011200
SUMMARY:Attention can descend
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nThe work is not necessarily to climb into ever more complex thought\, but to sink beneath the demand to conceptualize everything.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.\n\nConnection: Cage's silence and the Cloud author's unknowing both change attention by removing expected content. One opens the ear\; the other interrupts conceptual possession.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270213@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270213T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270213T011200
SUMMARY:Loss can alter value
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nSomething relinquished can acquire a different meaning after it is no longer available for possession.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270214@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270214T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270214T011200
SUMMARY:The heart has chambers
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nInterior life is not a single transparent space. Contradictory forces can inhabit the same person.\n\nContext: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270215@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270215T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270215T011200
SUMMARY:God is not your concept
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nThe concept of God can become an idol precisely when it feels most intellectually secure.\n\nContext: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270216@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270216T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270216T011200
SUMMARY:Impersonality can protect
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nThe deepest value in a person may be obscured when everything is filtered through status\, personality\, and social recognition.\n\nContext: late essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270217@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270217T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270217T011200
SUMMARY:The small can hold all
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nA tiny and vulnerable thing can disclose a metaphysical horizon vastly larger than its apparent scale.\n\nContext: Revelations of Divine Love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270218@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270218T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270218T011200
SUMMARY:The comic can be severe
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nComedy can expose spiritual disproportion more mercilessly than solemnity.\n\nContext: fiction and essays.\n\nConnection: Weil's attention and O'Connor's violence look opposed\, yet both are suspicious of the self-protective stories by which a person avoids reality.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270219@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270219T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270219T011200
SUMMARY:Strength can become gentleness
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nThe capacities once used for domination can be redirected rather than merely suppressed.\n\nContext: desert tradition surrounding Moses.\n\nConnection: O'Connor and Abba Moses both distrust moral superiority. One uses grotesque fiction\, the other comic desert gestures\, to expose the person who can see everyone else's fault.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270220@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270220T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270220T011200
SUMMARY:Choose\, then become
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nA choice is not only a selection among options\; serious choices participate in forming the chooser.\n\nContext: Either/Or.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270221@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270221T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270221T011200
SUMMARY:Failure can perform
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nAn apparent failure of entertainment can itself become the performance by exposing what an audience believes it is owed.\n\nContext: performance practice.\n\nConnection: Kaufman destabilizes the audience's certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard destabilizes the individual's certainty about borrowed conviction. Both make the observer responsible for deciding where to stand.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270222@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270222T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270222T011200
SUMMARY:Night is not nothing
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nPeriods of obscurity can dismantle inadequate forms of knowing without yet supplying replacements.\n\nContext: Dark Night.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270223@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270223T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270223T011200
SUMMARY:Structure distributes force
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nStrength can emerge from how forces are distributed rather than from simply adding more material.\n\nContext: tensegrity and structural work.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270224@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270224T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270224T011200
SUMMARY:Small words can turn
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nA modest verbal turn near the end of a poem can reorganize everything that came before it.\n\nContext: The Temple.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270225@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270225T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270225T011200
SUMMARY:Consolation is not the goal
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nPleasant spiritual experience can become another possession if it is confused with the reality it mediates.\n\nContext: Ascent of Mount Carmel.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270226@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270226T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270226T011200
SUMMARY:Joy can be defiant
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nJoy need not result from naïveté\; it can be a stance maintained in full awareness of difficulty.\n\nContext: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270227@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270227T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270227T011200
SUMMARY:Moses never arrives
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nA spiritual journey can deepen precisely because its object is inexhaustible rather than finally possessed.\n\nContext: Life of Moses.\n\nConnection: Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity with absence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270228@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270228T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270228T011200
SUMMARY:Names reveal and hide
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nA name can genuinely disclose while remaining radically inadequate to what it names.\n\nContext: Divine Names.\n\nConnection: Kaufman's refusal to clarify whether the performance is 'really' a performance has an odd kinship with apophatic refusal: certainty about the frame is withheld\, though for radically different ends.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270301@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270301T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270301T011200
SUMMARY:Forget to attend
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nDeliberate forgetting can sometimes clear the field for a different mode of attention.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270302@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270302T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270302T011200
SUMMARY:Habit can divide the will
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nOne can sincerely desire change while habits embody older desires strongly enough to make the will feel split.\n\nContext: Confessions\, Book VIII.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270303@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270303T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270303T011200
SUMMARY:The environment stays hidden
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nThe most powerful media effects are often difficult to notice because they become the background conditions of ordinary perception.\n\nContext: The Medium Is the Massage.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270304@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270304T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270304T011200
SUMMARY:Attention can be guarded
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nAttention is treated as something with thresholds and gates\, not as a neutral spotlight one always controls.\n\nContext: ascetical tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270305@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270305T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270305T011200
SUMMARY:Chance can compose
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nChance procedures can remove habits of preference and allow structures the maker would not deliberately choose.\n\nContext: chance operations and Music of Changes.\n\nConnection: Cage's silence and the Cloud author's unknowing both change attention by removing expected content. One opens the ear\; the other interrupts conceptual possession.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270306@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270306T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270306T011200
SUMMARY:The whole changes parts
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nIn a system\, the behavior of a part depends on relationships that disappear when the part is considered alone.\n\nContext: synergetics.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270307@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270307T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270307T011200
SUMMARY:Hierarchy can transmit
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nA hierarchy\, in Dionysius's ideal sense\, is a pattern of receiving and transmitting illumination rather than merely ranking status.\n\nContext: Celestial Hierarchy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270308@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270308T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270308T011200
SUMMARY:Ecstasy needs fruit
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nExtraordinary experience matters less than the durable transformation it produces in conduct and relation.\n\nContext: Interior Castle.\n\nConnection: Teresa's rooms and Evagrius's thought-patterns offer two different maps of interior attention: one architectural and relational\, one diagnostic and ascetic.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270309@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270309T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270309T011200
SUMMARY:Discern the right burden
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nNot every hardship should be escaped\, and not every hardship should be embraced. Discernment asks what a difficulty is doing.\n\nContext: Sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270310@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270310T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270310T011200
SUMMARY:Repetition is not return
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nTrue repetition is not simple recurrence. What returns can become new because the person returning has changed.\n\nContext: Repetition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270311@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270311T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270311T011200
SUMMARY:Attention is generosity
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nTo attend without immediately appropriating\, categorizing\, or fixing another can itself be a form of generosity.\n\nContext: essays on attention.\n\nConnection: Weil's attention and Cage's listening meet in a disciplined refusal to decide too quickly what matters. Weil makes it ethical\; Cage makes it acoustic.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270312@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270312T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270312T011200
SUMMARY:Use is not enjoyment
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nConfusing instrumental use with final enjoyment distorts both objects and relationships.\n\nContext: On Christian Doctrine.\n\nConnection: Augustine's divided will and Kierkegaard's analysis of despair both make the self a task rather than a transparent possession.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270313@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270313T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270313T011200
SUMMARY:Oppression enters vision
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nSocial domination changes imagination itself\, teaching people to perceive constraint as natural.\n\nContext: prophetic writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270314@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270314T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270314T011200
SUMMARY:Every medium retrieves
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nNew media often revive something older that a previous environment had suppressed or made obsolete.\n\nContext: Laws of Media.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270315@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270315T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270315T011200
SUMMARY:The audience is material
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nReaction is not merely a response to the work\; it can be one of the materials from which the work is made.\n\nContext: performance practice.\n\nConnection: Kaufman destabilizes the audience's certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard destabilizes the individual's certainty about borrowed conviction. Both make the observer responsible for deciding where to stand.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270316@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270316T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270316T011200
SUMMARY:Extensions become amputations
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nAn extension of one capacity can numb or diminish another\; augmentation always has a sensory price.\n\nContext: Understanding Media.\n\nConnection: McLuhan's figure/ground distinction becomes almost theatrical in Kaufman\, whose performances make the audience discover that its own expectations were part of the medium.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270317@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270317T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270317T011200
SUMMARY:Inner poverty can speak
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nNeed can become perceptible only when the self stops covering it with competence and self-explanation.\n\nContext: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270318@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270318T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270318T011200
SUMMARY:Contradiction can birth
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nSome forms of generativity arise not after contradiction disappears but through the structured tension it creates.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.\n\nConnection: Blake read Böhme\, and the affinity shows: both treat contraries and energetic tension as generative rather than as defects to be simply erased.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270319@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270319T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270319T011200
SUMMARY:Surprise keeps play alive
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nA living game must leave room for outcomes its players could not fully script in advance.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.\n\nConnection: Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final\, stable verdict about what game was being played.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270320@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270320T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270320T011200
SUMMARY:The desert magnifies
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nReduction of external stimulation can make small interior movements feel larger and therefore easier to see.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270321@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270321T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270321T011200
SUMMARY:Strength invites response
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nStrength differs from power when it enlarges the capacity of others to answer rather than reducing them to compliance.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.\n\nConnection: Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final\, stable verdict about what game was being played.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270322@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270322T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270322T011200
SUMMARY:Discern before fleeing
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nThe urge to escape can itself become material for discernment rather than an automatic instruction.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270323@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270323T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270323T011200
SUMMARY:The ordinary can answer
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nDomestic and commonplace things can become intellectually and spiritually articulate under sustained attention.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270324@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270324T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270324T011200
SUMMARY:The child is not simpler
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nWriting for children need not flatten complexity\; children can encounter mystery before they possess adult explanatory language.\n\nContext: essays and fiction.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270325@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270325T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270325T011200
SUMMARY:Repetition changes an image
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nReturning to the same face\, motif\, or poem can make repetition cumulative rather than redundant.\n\nContext: artistic practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270326@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270326T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270326T011200
SUMMARY:Violence can disclose
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nIn O'Connor's fiction\, violence often strips away a character's preferred account of reality rather than merely supplying plot excitement.\n\nContext: Mystery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270327@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270327T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270327T011200
SUMMARY:Fairy tales keep doors
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nA fantastic story can preserve openings that explanation would prematurely close.\n\nContext: The Fantastic Imagination.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270328@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270328T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270328T011200
SUMMARY:Taste can be a cage
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nPreference helps us navigate\, but Cage repeatedly asks what becomes audible when taste stops policing experience.\n\nContext: Silence.\n\nConnection: Cage and Herbert could hardly sound less alike\, yet both make form itself carry thought: Cage through procedural listening\, Herbert through shaped language.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270329@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270329T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270329T011200
SUMMARY:Hope is socially dangerous
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nHope becomes political whenever it refuses to let an existing order identify itself with the limits of the possible.\n\nContext: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270330@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270330T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270330T011200
SUMMARY:A center can be nowhere
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nThinking about infinity can unsettle ordinary spatial intuitions about center\, boundary\, and location.\n\nContext: cosmological writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270331@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270331T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270331T011200
SUMMARY:Complaint can become prayer
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nA poem can begin in resistance\, irritation\, or argument and let that movement itself become a form of address.\n\nContext: The Temple.\n\nConnection: Cage and Herbert could hardly sound less alike\, yet both make form itself carry thought: Cage through procedural listening\, Herbert through shaped language.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270401@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270401T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270401T011200
SUMMARY:A violent past can change
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nA person's history need not be denied in order for transformation to be real\; the past can remain part of the story without dictating its end.\n\nContext: Moses's desert tradition.\n\nConnection: O'Connor and Abba Moses both distrust moral superiority. One uses grotesque fiction\, the other comic desert gestures\, to expose the person who can see everyone else's fault.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270402@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270402T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270402T011200
SUMMARY:Listening has no center
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nA field of sound need not have a privileged focal point. Attention can move without requiring hierarchy.\n\nContext: experimental music practice.\n\nConnection: Cage and Herbert could hardly sound less alike\, yet both make form itself carry thought: Cage through procedural listening\, Herbert through shaped language.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270403@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270403T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270403T011200
SUMMARY:Judgment can soften
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nThe desire to judge another can be displaced by awareness of shared fragility.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270404@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270404T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270404T011200
SUMMARY:Tradition gives the dead votes
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nTradition can be understood as extending participation across generations rather than letting the present monopolize authority.\n\nContext: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270405@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270405T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270405T011200
SUMMARY:The line can be prophetic
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nGraphic form\, text\, and image can participate in one visionary act rather than serving separate illustrative functions.\n\nContext: illuminated books.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270406@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270406T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270406T011200
SUMMARY:Despair can look normal
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nA person can function successfully while remaining estranged from the self they are becoming.\n\nContext: The Sickness Unto Death.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270407@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270407T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270407T011200
SUMMARY:A polygon seeks the circle
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nThe image of a many-sided polygon approaching a circle shows how approximation can increase without becoming identity.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270408@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270408T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270408T011200
SUMMARY:Practice alters perception
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nAscetic practice changes what appears desirable\, possible\, and real\, not merely what behavior is permitted.\n\nContext: Four Hundred Texts on Love.\n\nConnection: Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion meet in a refusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270409@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270409T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270409T011200
SUMMARY:Reading can transform
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nInterpretation is not only extraction of information. The reader can be changed by the act of reading.\n\nContext: biblical exegesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270410@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270410T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270410T011200
SUMMARY:Become singular
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nA crowd can lend borrowed conviction. Becoming a self requires personally inhabiting what one takes to be true.\n\nContext: writings on the single individual.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270411@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270411T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270411T011200
SUMMARY:A cell mirrors the mind
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nConfinement can make inner agitation more visible because fewer external changes are available to disguise it.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270412@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270412T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270412T011200
SUMMARY:Power ends play
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nPower can force an outcome\, but forcing an outcome is different from sustaining a field in which genuine play remains possible.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270413@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270413T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270413T011200
SUMMARY:Noise can become identity
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nConstant stimulation can become a defense against encountering the self that appears when performance stops.\n\nContext: contemplative writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270414@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270414T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270414T011200
SUMMARY:Patience can be active
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nPatience need not mean passivity. It can be the disciplined refusal to let discomfort dictate the next move.\n\nContext: sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270415@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270415T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270415T011200
SUMMARY:Work narrows the horizon
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nA life understood only through work and utility can conceal dimensions of existence disclosed by play\, love\, death\, and festival.\n\nContext: existential phenomenology.\n\nConnection: Carse and Fink both take play seriously enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes continuation\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270416@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270416T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270416T011200
SUMMARY:Punishment need not define
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nDivine judgment can be interpreted within a larger horizon of healing rather than as an ultimate delight in retribution.\n\nContext: Isaac's eschatological writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270417@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270417T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270417T011200
SUMMARY:Edges can gain thickness
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA living boundary often becomes a zone with its own structure rather than a thin line separating two domains.\n\nContext: The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Fink's world-horizon and Alexander's field of centers both resist treating things as isolated units. Each asks what larger order lets a thing be what it is.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270418@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270418T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270418T011200
SUMMARY:Joy can anticipate truth
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nCelebration can be anticipatory rather than escapist: a bodily rehearsal of a world not yet fully present.\n\nContext: Theology of Play and related essays.\n\nConnection: Moltmann's future-oriented hope and Carse's infinite play both resist closure. One is theological and eschatological\; the other is ludic\, but both ask whether the future remains genuinely open.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270419@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270419T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270419T011200
SUMMARY:Interior space is large
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nThe inner life can be approached as a complex architecture rather than a single stream of private feeling.\n\nContext: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270420@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270420T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270420T011200
SUMMARY:Systems hide in objects
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nAn object often makes sense only as part of a larger system of flows\, constraints\, and relations.\n\nContext: synergetics and design science.\n\nConnection: Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270421@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270421T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270421T011200
SUMMARY:Watch before arguing
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nA thought can be observed as it forms\, before one accepts its framing and begins debating inside it.\n\nContext: ascetical writings.\n\nConnection: Teresa's rooms and Evagrius's thought-patterns offer two different maps of interior attention: one architectural and relational\, one diagnostic and ascetic.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270422@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270422T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270422T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can become clutter
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nMore concepts are not always more contact\; intellectual accumulation can become a defense against encounter.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270423@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270423T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270423T011200
SUMMARY:Do not judge too quickly
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nAwareness of one's own disorder can interrupt the pleasure of judging another person's visible failure.\n\nContext: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270424@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270424T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270424T011200
SUMMARY:Love joins without blur
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nUnion need not abolish particularity. Relation can intensify difference while overcoming estrangement.\n\nContext: Ambigua.\n\nConnection: Alexander's wholeness and Maximus's communion share a structural intuition: unity is strongest when differences are related without being flattened.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270425@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270425T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270425T011200
SUMMARY:Waiting changes desire
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nDelay can transform not only whether a desire is fulfilled but what the desiring person becomes.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270426@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270426T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270426T011200
SUMMARY:Tears enlarge perception
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nCompunction is not merely private sadness\; it can become a way of perceiving the vulnerability of the whole creation.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.\n\nConnection: Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion meet in a refusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270427@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270427T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270427T011200
SUMMARY:Restlessness can guide
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nRestlessness is not only a defect to suppress\; it can reveal that a desire has mistaken a finite object for its final home.\n\nContext: Confessions.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270428@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270428T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270428T011200
SUMMARY:Silence can carry speech
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nWhat a poem withholds can structure meaning as strongly as what it states.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270429@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270429T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270429T011200
SUMMARY:Attachment can hide
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nEven attachment to good experiences can narrow perception when the experience becomes an end in itself.\n\nContext: Ascent of Mount Carmel.\n\nConnection: Rossetti's renunciation and John's detachment both ask whether desire can be purified by relinquishment\, though their poetic and mystical registers remain distinct.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270430@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270430T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270430T011200
SUMMARY:Festival changes time
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nFestival interrupts instrumental time. Its value lies partly in suspending the demand that every moment justify itself by what it produces.\n\nContext: writings on play and world.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270501@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270501T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270501T011200
SUMMARY:Desire gives weight
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nWhat we love gives direction and gravity to attention\, memory\, and action.\n\nContext: Confessions.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270502@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270502T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270502T011200
SUMMARY:Compassion reaches animals
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nIsaac's famous image of the merciful heart includes sorrow for humans\, animals\, and all creation.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270503@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270503T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270503T011200
SUMMARY:Growth can stay coherent
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nComplex order can emerge incrementally when each step preserves and strengthens what is already alive.\n\nContext: The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Fink's world-horizon and Alexander's field of centers both resist treating things as isolated units. Each asks what larger order lets a thing be what it is.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270504@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270504T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270504T011200
SUMMARY:Restraint can intensify
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nFormal and emotional restraint can concentrate rather than diminish expressive force.\n\nContext: poetry.\n\nConnection: Rossetti's renunciation and John's detachment both ask whether desire can be purified by relinquishment\, though their poetic and mystical registers remain distinct.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270505@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270505T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270505T011200
SUMMARY:Infinity changes desire
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nIf the good is infinite\, desire need not end in possession\; desire itself can become a form of participation.\n\nContext: Life of Moses.\n\nConnection: Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity with absence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270506@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270506T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270506T011200
SUMMARY:Affliction changes scale
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nExtreme suffering can alter the very structure through which a person experiences time\, self\, and world.\n\nContext: writings on affliction.\n\nConnection: Weil and Isaac both stretch attention beyond the self's preferred circle. Weil emphasizes the reality of the afflicted other\; Isaac lets compassion widen toward all creation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270507@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270507T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270507T011200
SUMMARY:Make the next small step
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nLarge coherent structures can arise from many local transformations when each step repairs the whole instead of imposing a complete blueprint.\n\nContext: The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Fink's world-horizon and Alexander's field of centers both resist treating things as isolated units. Each asks what larger order lets a thing be what it is.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270508@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270508T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270508T011200
SUMMARY:Time lives in attention
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nPast and future are encountered through present acts of memory and expectation.\n\nContext: Confessions\, Book XI.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270509@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270509T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270509T011200
SUMMARY:Fire can purify
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nImages of divine fire can be read as transformative rather than merely retaliatory.\n\nContext: homilies and theological writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270510@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270510T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270510T011200
SUMMARY:Tools can reverse ends
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nTools and institutions can pass a threshold where producing more of a service begins to frustrate the human good they were built to serve.\n\nContext: Tools for Conviviality\; Medical Nemesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270511@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270511T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270511T011200
SUMMARY:Imagination rehearses freedom
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nImagined worlds can loosen the sense that present arrangements exhaust the forms life might take.\n\nContext: fantasy writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270512@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270512T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270512T011200
SUMMARY:The heart can widen
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nInterior transformation can enlarge the capacity to hold others rather than merely perfect a private self.\n\nContext: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270513@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270513T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270513T011200
SUMMARY:A tale should breathe
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nA story can be damaged by over-explanation when commentary closes the imaginative space the tale created.\n\nContext: The Fantastic Imagination.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270514@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270514T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270514T011200
SUMMARY:Stay long enough to see
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nRemaining with a place\, practice\, or difficulty can reveal patterns that novelty continually resets before they become legible.\n\nContext: sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270515@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270515T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270515T011200
SUMMARY:Reality can be rehearsed
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nRepeated performance can make an invented situation socially real enough that its consequences no longer depend on the original fiction.\n\nContext: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270516@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270516T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270516T011200
SUMMARY:Darkness gives light contour
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nLight becomes manifest against differentiation and resistance\; pure undifferentiated light would disclose no form.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270517@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270517T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270517T011200
SUMMARY:Mortality sharpens beauty
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nBeauty can intensify under awareness that it cannot be possessed indefinitely.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270518@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270518T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270518T011200
SUMMARY:A boundary can move
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nFinite play protects boundaries\; infinite play can treat boundaries as provisional features of an ongoing field.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.\n\nConnection: Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final\, stable verdict about what game was being played.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270519@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270519T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270519T011200
SUMMARY:The map may need burning
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nA guide useful for one stage can become an obstacle when mistaken for the terrain itself.\n\nContext: Ascent of Mount Carmel.\n\nConnection: Rossetti's renunciation and John's detachment both ask whether desire can be purified by relinquishment\, though their poetic and mystical registers remain distinct.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270520@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270520T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270520T011200
SUMMARY:Mercy can interrupt law
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nRules matter\, but the desert tradition repeatedly asks whether applying a rule in this moment serves restoration or self-righteousness.\n\nContext: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270521@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270521T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270521T011200
SUMMARY:Revision becomes a second work
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nA heavily revised work may cease to be merely corrected and become a different solution to the same imaginative problem.\n\nContext: poetic practice.\n\nConnection: The Rossettis share an intense symbolic imagination\, but Dante Gabriel often lets desire saturate the image while Christina repeatedly tests desire through restraint\, waiting\, and renunciation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270522@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270522T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270522T011200
SUMMARY:The future can reorder love
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nA hoped-for future can alter what present desire is willing to surrender.\n\nContext: devotional poetry.\n\nConnection: Rossetti's renunciation and John's detachment both ask whether desire can be purified by relinquishment\, though their poetic and mystical registers remain distinct.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270523@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270523T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270523T011200
SUMMARY:Let intention loosen
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nReducing control does not mean abandoning craft. It can mean designing procedures that admit events the ego would have excluded.\n\nContext: chance procedures.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270524@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270524T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270524T011200
SUMMARY:Freedom risks selfhood
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nSelfhood requires a kind of contraction and differentiation\, but that same movement can harden into isolation.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270525@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270525T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270525T011200
SUMMARY:Mercy exceeds measure
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nCompassion can refuse the arithmetic by which worthiness is calculated.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270526@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270526T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270526T011200
SUMMARY:Text can frame vision
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nA sonnet attached to an image can redirect the viewer's attention without exhausting the image's ambiguity.\n\nContext: The House of Life and related works.\n\nConnection: The Rossettis share an intense symbolic imagination\, but Dante Gabriel often lets desire saturate the image while Christina repeatedly tests desire through restraint\, waiting\, and renunciation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270527@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270527T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270527T011200
SUMMARY:Prototype the future
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nA working artifact can sometimes test a possibility more honestly than argument alone.\n\nContext: design practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270528@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270528T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270528T011200
SUMMARY:Sin is not the last fact
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nJulian refuses to deny sin while also refusing to treat it as the ultimate interpretive key to reality.\n\nContext: Revelations.\n\nConnection: Julian and Isaac share a startling confidence in mercy without making suffering imaginary. Both press compassion beyond the punitive instincts of ordinary moral accounting.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270529@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270529T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270529T011200
SUMMARY:A face can become emblem
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nPortraiture can move from likeness toward symbolic concentration\, making a face carry more than biography.\n\nContext: painting practice.\n\nConnection: The Rossettis share an intense symbolic imagination\, but Dante Gabriel often lets desire saturate the image while Christina repeatedly tests desire through restraint\, waiting\, and renunciation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270530@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270530T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270530T011200
SUMMARY:Meaning can ripen later
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nAn image encountered before it is understood may keep working until later experience makes it newly intelligible.\n\nContext: essays on imagination.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270531@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270531T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270531T011200
SUMMARY:Souls are educable
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nSpiritual life can be imagined pedagogically: error\, correction\, purification\, and growth rather than static classification.\n\nContext: homilies and speculative theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270601@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270601T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270601T011200
SUMMARY:Why matters less than how
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nAn action done from freedom can differ radically from the same external action done from possessiveness or self-display.\n\nContext: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270602@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270602T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270602T011200
SUMMARY:Space can be positive
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nEmpty space becomes powerful when it has definite shape and presence rather than remaining leftover area between objects.\n\nContext: A Pattern Language\; The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270603@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270603T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270603T011200
SUMMARY:Grace is not niceness
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nGrace does not necessarily preserve dignity\, comfort\, or self-image. Its arrival may feel like catastrophe to the person being changed.\n\nContext: fiction.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270604@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270604T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270604T011200
SUMMARY:Comparison distorts effort
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nAnother person's discipline can become a distraction when copied without regard to one's own capacities and temptations.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270605@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270605T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270605T011200
SUMMARY:Vision needs distance
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nA writer may need enough distance from familiar surroundings to perceive their peculiar structure rather than reproduce local clichés.\n\nContext: Mystery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270606@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270606T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270606T011200
SUMMARY:The past can be invented
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nHistorical imagery can be less a reconstruction of the past than a newly made symbolic environment.\n\nContext: Pre-Raphaelite practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270607@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270607T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270607T011200
SUMMARY:Love needs no audience
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nAn action performed for recognition differs inwardly from one whose value does not depend on being witnessed.\n\nContext: New Seeds of Contemplation.\n\nConnection: Merton's false self and Eckhart's detachment converge in suspicion of the identity that must constantly possess\, perform\, and defend itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270608@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270608T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270608T011200
SUMMARY:Titles freeze the past
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nA title names a completed victory. When identity rests on titles\, the past begins governing what one is allowed to become.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270609@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270609T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270609T011200
SUMMARY:Clarity follows simplification
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nReducing compulsive mental traffic can make perception more exact rather than merely more peaceful.\n\nContext: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270610@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270610T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270610T011200
SUMMARY:Image answers poem
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nWhen image and poem address the same subject\, neither need merely illustrate the other\; each can alter how the other is read.\n\nContext: double works and sonnet-painting relations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270611@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270611T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270611T011200
SUMMARY:Play makes a world
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nPlay is not merely an escape from reality. It opens a distinctive world with its own seriousness\, rules\, and horizon.\n\nContext: Play as Symbol of the World.\n\nConnection: Carse and Fink both take play seriously enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes continuation\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270612@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270612T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270612T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can be simpler
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nA single undivided intention can sometimes reach further than a proliferation of spiritual techniques.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270613@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270613T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270613T011200
SUMMARY:The self is received
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nThe deepest self is not manufactured by accumulating distinctions\; it is discovered through a release of compulsive self-construction.\n\nContext: New Seeds of Contemplation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270614@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270614T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270614T011200
SUMMARY:Seeing can be gentle
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nA severe reality need not require a severe mode of attention.\n\nContext: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270615@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270615T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270615T011200
SUMMARY:Darkness can disclose
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nAt a limit of knowledge\, obscurity need not mean absence. It can mark encounter with what exceeds comprehension.\n\nContext: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270616@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270616T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270616T011200
SUMMARY:The senses have limits
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nA mode of knowing can be genuine and still reach a point beyond which a different mode is required.\n\nContext: Ascent of Mount Carmel.\n\nConnection: Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity with absence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270617@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270617T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270617T011200
SUMMARY:Community tests solitude
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nDesert spirituality is not purely solitary\; relations with other people expose what private discipline can hide.\n\nContext: Sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270618@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270618T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270618T011200
SUMMARY:Constraint can sing
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nFormal limits can generate invention rather than merely suppress it.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270619@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270619T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270619T011200
SUMMARY:Division seeks communion
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nDifference need not culminate in separation\; distinctions can become sites of communion without being erased.\n\nContext: Ambigua.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270620@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270620T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270620T011200
SUMMARY:The birth is inward
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nEckhart repeatedly treats divine birth not only as a past event but as something that must occur in the ground of the soul.\n\nContext: Christmas sermons.\n\nConnection: Merton's false self and Eckhart's detachment converge in suspicion of the identity that must constantly possess\, perform\, and defend itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270621@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270621T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270621T011200
SUMMARY:Solitude can join
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nSolitude need not mean isolation\; it can expose forms of relation obscured by constant social performance.\n\nContext: contemplative writings.\n\nConnection: Merton's false self and Eckhart's detachment converge in suspicion of the identity that must constantly possess\, perform\, and defend itself.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270622@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270622T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270622T011200
SUMMARY:Absence refines desire
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nThe loss of familiar consolation can expose whether desire seeks reality or merely a preferred experience of it.\n\nContext: Dark Night.\n\nConnection: Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity with absence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270623@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270623T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270623T011200
SUMMARY:A short word can gather
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nA very small prayer can concentrate desire more effectively than an elaborate explanation.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270624@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270624T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270624T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty draws beyond itself
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nBeauty can awaken desire that refuses to terminate in the beautiful object as possession.\n\nContext: Homilies on the Song of Songs.\n\nConnection: Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity with absence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270625@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270625T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270625T011200
SUMMARY:Climb by unknowing
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nThe ascent of thought may culminate not in a larger concept but in relinquishing conceptual possession.\n\nContext: Mystical Theology.\n\nConnection: Eckhart's detachment and Dionysius's negation share an apophatic discipline: even a true concept can become false in use when held as possession.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270626@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270626T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270626T011200
SUMMARY:Need is not the good
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nNecessity governs much of the world\, but recognizing necessity is not the same as worshiping it.\n\nContext: Gravity and Grace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270627@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270627T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270627T011200
SUMMARY:You can trim without loss
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nEfficiency can mean preserving function while removing unnecessary mass\, friction\, or duplication.\n\nContext: design science.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270628@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270628T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270628T011200
SUMMARY:Contraries share a room
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nHoliness and disorder can coexist in one person without either being imaginary.\n\nContext: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270629@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270629T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270629T011200
SUMMARY:Notice the ground
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nWe habitually notice the figure and miss the environment that makes the figure possible.\n\nContext: figure/ground analysis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270630@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270630T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270630T011200
SUMMARY:Excess can look dark
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nWhat exceeds cognition can appear as darkness\, not because nothing is there but because the instrument is overwhelmed.\n\nContext: Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270701@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270701T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270701T011200
SUMMARY:Love is epistemic
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nHow one loves affects what one can know\; distorted relation can become distorted perception.\n\nContext: Four Hundred Texts on Love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270702@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270702T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270702T011200
SUMMARY:Suffering forbids closure
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nAny account of reality that makes suffering feel final has surrendered something essential to hope.\n\nContext: The Crucified God.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270703@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270703T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270703T011200
SUMMARY:A game exceeds players
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nOnce play begins\, the field can generate possibilities no individual player fully controls.\n\nContext: Play as Symbol of the World.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270704@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270704T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270704T011200
SUMMARY:Nothing need be added
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nAn environment already contains events. Art can sometimes consist in changing the conditions of attention rather than adding an object.\n\nContext: 4′33″ and later work.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270705@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270705T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270705T011200
SUMMARY:Death frames the world
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nMortality is not simply one event among others\; awareness of death alters how the whole field of life appears.\n\nContext: Existence and Coexistence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270706@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270706T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270706T011200
SUMMARY:Poetry precedes proof
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nSome insight arrives before discursive proof\, through intuition embodied in image\, rhythm\, and making.\n\nContext: Creative Intuition in Art and Poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270707@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270707T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270707T011200
SUMMARY:Light needs distinction
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nManifestation requires difference: an undifferentiated brightness would reveal nothing in particular.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270708@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270708T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270708T011200
SUMMARY:Name the thought-pattern
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nSeeing a thought as a recurring kind of event can loosen its claim to be simply identical with reality.\n\nContext: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270709@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270709T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270709T011200
SUMMARY:A world fits a grain
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nScale in imagination is unstable\; the minute can become an aperture onto the immense.\n\nContext: Auguries of Innocence.\n\nConnection: Blake read Böhme\, and the affinity shows: both treat contraries and energetic tension as generative rather than as defects to be simply erased.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270710@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270710T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270710T011200
SUMMARY:Fantasy can tell truth
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nDeparting from ordinary realism can reveal structures of desire and moral imagination that literal description misses.\n\nContext: fantasy writings.\n\nConnection: MacDonald and Blake both refuse to treat imagination as decorative unreality. For both\, imaginative vision can expose possibilities the ordinary habits of perception conceal.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270711@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270711T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270711T011200
SUMMARY:Stillness needs training
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nQuiet is not merely an absence of activity. It can require disciplined attention to what otherwise seizes the mind.\n\nContext: ascetical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270712@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270712T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270712T011200
SUMMARY:The person exceeds the role
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nA person participates in communities without being reducible to function\, class\, usefulness\, or collective identity.\n\nContext: The Person and the Common Good.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270713@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270713T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270713T011200
SUMMARY:Holding is not owning
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nTo hold something in attention can differ radically from possessing or mastering it.\n\nContext: hazelnut vision.\n\nConnection: Julian's hazelnut and Blake's grain of sand both let the minute open onto the cosmic. Their metaphysics differ\, but the change of scale is striking.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270714@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270714T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270714T011200
SUMMARY:Form can think
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nThe shape\, rhythm\, and arrangement of language can carry an argument that paraphrase alone cannot preserve.\n\nContext: pattern poems and devotional poetry.\n\nConnection: Maritain's claim that art knows through making becomes concrete in Herbert\, where poetic form does intellectual work that paraphrase cannot replace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270715@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270715T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270715T011200
SUMMARY:Limits make a shape
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nA boundary can create the conditions for form\, play\, and meaning rather than merely restricting them.\n\nContext: Orthodoxy and essays.\n\nConnection: Chesterton inherits much from MacDonald's imaginative Christianity: wonder is not childish simplification but a discipline for seeing the ordinary as contingent and strange.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270716@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270716T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270716T011200
SUMMARY:The false self performs
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nA socially manufactured identity can become so fluent that its performance is mistaken for the whole person.\n\nContext: New Seeds of Contemplation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270717@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270717T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270717T011200
SUMMARY:A merciful heart widens
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nCompassion can expand beyond one's preferred circle until alien suffering becomes difficult to exclude.\n\nContext: Ascetical Homilies.\n\nConnection: Julian and Isaac share a startling confidence in mercy without making suffering imaginary. Both press compassion beyond the punitive instincts of ordinary moral accounting.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270718@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270718T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270718T011200
SUMMARY:Resistance can educate
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nA difficulty can become diagnostic: not because suffering is inherently good\, but because resistance reveals where freedom is thin.\n\nContext: sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270719@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270719T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270719T011200
SUMMARY:Simplicity removes excuses
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nA stripped-down environment reduces the number of external explanations available for one's own agitation.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270720@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270720T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270720T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can argue with itself
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nA poem can let conflicting desires speak without immediately resolving which voice is the 'real' one.\n\nContext: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270721@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270721T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270721T011200
SUMMARY:Light can blind
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nToo much light can be phenomenologically equivalent to darkness when the perceiver cannot accommodate its intensity.\n\nContext: Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270722@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270722T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270722T011200
SUMMARY:Experience has texture
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nSpiritual language becomes concrete in the Macarian tradition: warmth\, fire\, sweetness\, struggle\, weight\, and movement.\n\nContext: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270723@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270723T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270723T011200
SUMMARY:Vision changes objects
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nThe world encountered is partly shaped by the quality and habits of perception brought to it.\n\nContext: prophetic writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270724@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270724T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270724T011200
SUMMARY:The cosmos is relational
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nCreated things have meaning not only as isolated entities but through the relations that gather them into a larger order.\n\nContext: Ambigua.\n\nConnection: Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion meet in a refusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270725@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270725T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270725T011200
SUMMARY:Means educate desire
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nThe means used toward an end reshape the agent pursuing it\, and can quietly alter the end itself.\n\nContext: political and moral philosophy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270726@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270726T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270726T011200
SUMMARY:Experience needs discernment
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nIntensity does not authenticate an experience. Teresa repeatedly tests experiences by their effects and coherence.\n\nContext: Life\; Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270727@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270727T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270727T011200
SUMMARY:The toy is double
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nA toy is both an ordinary object and\, within play\, something transformed by the world the players inhabit.\n\nContext: Play as Symbol of the World.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270728@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270728T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270728T011200
SUMMARY:Wonder needs familiarity
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nThe ordinary can become astonishing when familiar existence is seen as something that need not have been.\n\nContext: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270729@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270729T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270729T011200
SUMMARY:The shadow has news
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nRejected psychic material often returns indirectly. What disturbs the self-image may contain information the self needs.\n\nContext: shadow and individuation.\n\nConnection: Jung and Teresa both imagine interior life spatially. Teresa's castle is theological and ordered toward prayer\; Jung's psyche is analytic and symbolic. The shared architecture makes the difference more interesting\, not less.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270730@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270730T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270730T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can deepen mystery
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nGreater knowledge need not reduce mystery. It can increase awareness of what exceeds knowledge.\n\nContext: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270731@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270731T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270731T011200
SUMMARY:Small acts expose worlds
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nA seemingly minor practice can reveal the structure of desires that abstract reflection leaves hidden.\n\nContext: Macarian tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270801@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270801T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270801T011200
SUMMARY:Prayer can be practical
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nContemplative practice is tested in ordinary relations\, not merely in intensity of experience.\n\nContext: Interior Castle.\n\nConnection: Jung and Teresa both imagine interior life spatially. Teresa's castle is theological and ordered toward prayer\; Jung's psyche is analytic and symbolic. The shared architecture makes the difference more interesting\, not less.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270802@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270802T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270802T011200
SUMMARY:Good form has roughness
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nExact regularity can weaken life when it suppresses local adaptation. Roughness can be evidence of responsive order.\n\nContext: The Nature of Order.\n\nConnection: Fink's world-horizon and Alexander's field of centers both resist treating things as isolated units. Each asks what larger order lets a thing be what it is.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270803@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270803T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270803T011200
SUMMARY:Habits travel with you
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nA new place cannot by itself remove the patterns one carries into it.\n\nContext: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270804@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270804T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270804T011200
SUMMARY:Confession changes memory
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nRecounting a life can reorganize the meaning of past events without changing the events themselves.\n\nContext: Confessions.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270805@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270805T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270805T011200
SUMMARY:Wisdom may look comic
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nA deliberately absurd gesture can carry moral force when it disarms abstraction and makes a hidden contradiction visible.\n\nContext: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270806@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270806T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270806T011200
SUMMARY:Contemplation unmasks roles
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nSilence can expose how much identity depends on rehearsed roles and reflected approval.\n\nContext: New Seeds of Contemplation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270807@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270807T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270807T011200
SUMMARY:Difference survives union
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nThe strongest union is not fusion. Genuine communion preserves what makes the participants distinct.\n\nContext: Christological and cosmic theology.\n\nConnection: Alexander's wholeness and Maximus's communion share a structural intuition: unity is strongest when differences are related without being flattened.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270808@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270808T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270808T011200
SUMMARY:Negation is not nihilism
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nSaying what cannot be said is not the same as saying there is nothing there.\n\nContext: Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270809@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270809T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270809T011200
SUMMARY:Images can recruit desire
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nMental images can carry affective force that exceeds their apparent informational content.\n\nContext: ascetical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270810@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270810T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270810T011200
SUMMARY:Virtue has no ceiling
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nMoral growth has no final plateau where further transformation becomes unnecessary.\n\nContext: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270811@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270811T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270811T011200
SUMMARY:The path keeps opening
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nProgress can reveal new distances rather than closing them\, without making the journey futile.\n\nContext: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270812@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270812T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270812T011200
SUMMARY:Obscurity can be honest
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nNot understanding can be a more truthful stance than pretending an experience has become conceptually manageable.\n\nContext: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270813@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270813T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270813T011200
SUMMARY:Hope disputes the present
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nHope is not optimism about present trends. It lets a promised future contradict what the present declares inevitable.\n\nContext: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270814@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270814T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270814T011200
SUMMARY:Tension can make form
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nOpposition and contraction need not be mere defects. Tension can participate in the emergence of form.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270815@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270815T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270815T011200
SUMMARY:The end rereads the start
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA vision of final restoration can alter how creation\, judgment\, and history are understood from the beginning.\n\nContext: On First Principles.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270816@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270816T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270816T011200
SUMMARY:Difference can reveal unity
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nThe coincidence of opposites does not erase finite distinctions\; it changes the level at which their opposition is interpreted.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.\n\nConnection: Gregory's endless ascent and Cusa's learned ignorance both make intellectual progress compatible with increasing awareness of inexhaustibility.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270817@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270817T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270817T011200
SUMMARY:A thought has a sequence
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nTempting thoughts often develop in stages\; recognizing an early stage can matter more than winning an argument after fixation.\n\nContext: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270818@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270818T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270818T011200
SUMMARY:Hope can remain unfinished
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nJulian's confidence does not require a complete theory of how every contradiction will be resolved.\n\nContext: Revelations.\n\nConnection: Moltmann and Julian both let hope resist the apparent finality of suffering\, but neither needs to pretend the wound is unreal.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270819@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270819T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270819T011200
SUMMARY:The world is not elsewhere
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nContemplation is not necessarily escape from the world\; it can intensify participation in what is actually present.\n\nContext: later writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270820@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270820T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270820T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness keeps tension
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nWholeness is not spotless consistency but a capacity to hold previously disowned tensions without simple collapse.\n\nContext: individuation.\n\nConnection: Jung's complexes and Evagrius's recurring thoughts belong to different psychologies\, yet both treat mental events as patterns with a degree of autonomy rather than transparent expressions of the conscious self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270821@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270821T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270821T011200
SUMMARY:Persona is a useful mask
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nA social persona is necessary\, but trouble begins when the mask is mistaken for the whole person.\n\nContext: Two Essays on Analytical Psychology.\n\nConnection: Jung's complexes and Evagrius's recurring thoughts belong to different psychologies\, yet both treat mental events as patterns with a degree of autonomy rather than transparent expressions of the conscious self.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270822@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270822T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270822T011200
SUMMARY:Loss can simplify love
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nWhen supports disappear\, desire can become more exact about what it actually seeks.\n\nContext: Dark Night.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270823@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270823T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270823T011200
SUMMARY:The self is a landscape
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nInner life can be spatially imagined: rooms\, depths\, battlefields\, hidden regions\, and places not yet explored.\n\nContext: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270824@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270824T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270824T011200
SUMMARY:Harmony keeps tension
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nHarmony need not mean the absence of opposition\; it can mean oppositions held in a generative order.\n\nContext: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270825@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270825T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270825T011200
SUMMARY:Can the game stay open?
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nSome games seek a terminating victory\; others are played so that possibility and participation can continue.\n\nContext: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270826@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270826T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270826T011200
SUMMARY:Beware tidy goodness
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nMoral self-certainty can become a way of avoiding conversion.\n\nContext: fiction and essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270827@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270827T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270827T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing has an asymptote
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nKnowledge can approach its object more exactly while simultaneously clarifying the distance that remains.\n\nContext: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270828@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270828T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270828T011200
SUMMARY:Entertainment can refuse
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performer can use the stage to refuse the audience's preferred contract rather than satisfy it.\n\nContext: performance practice.\n\nConnection: Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final\, stable verdict about what game was being played.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270829@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270829T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270829T011200
SUMMARY:The castle has rooms
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nDifferent modes of awareness can coexist within one person without all being equally accessible at once.\n\nContext: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270830@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270830T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270830T011200
SUMMARY:Vulnerability can endure
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nThe created thing is fragile\, contingent\, and still held in being\; dependence is not identical with worthlessness.\n\nContext: hazelnut vision.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270831@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20260924T031500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270831T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270831T011200
SUMMARY:The self is not transparent
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nIntrospection does not make the self fully available to itself. Memory and motive exceed conscious inspection.\n\nContext: Confessions.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
